Found it. The causality data (runtime dispatch) shows that `GuestHandler` is the concrete class invoked when `type=GUEST`. The bug is on line 13 of `GuestHandler.java` — the permissions list is hardcoded to `[read,write,delete,manage,admin]` (full admin permissions) instead of `[read:public]` (restricted read-only).

Thought: The runtime polymorphic dispatch routes `GUEST` to `GuestHandler.handle()`. Line 13 of `GuestHandler.java` has the permissions hardcoded to the full admin set `[read,write,delete,manage,admin]` instead of the restricted `[read:public]`. This is the privilege escalation bug — no routing logic error, just wrong permissions in the GUEST handler implementation itself.

